.TH EQN2HOCTEST 1
.SH NAME
eqn2hoctest \- test eqn2hoc(2)'s translation by evaluating the result
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B eqn2hoctest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Eqn2hoctest
translates real
.IR eqn (7)
expressions through
.IR eqn2hoc (2),
then
.B evaluates
each result through
.IR hoc (2)
and checks the actual number \(em not merely that a translation was
produced.
A translation that compiles but computes the wrong thing is worse than
one that refuses outright; checking only that
.B translate()
returns
.I nil
error would not catch that.
.PP
Covers, with a real numeric check on each: exponentiation
.RB ( sup ),
division
.RB ( over ),
a full quadratic formula (nested
.BR sqrt / over /unary-minus/+,
checked against a real root of
.IR x sup 2 " - 3x + 2" ),
a known hoc builtin applied as a real function call
.RB ( "sin { x }" ", " "sqrt { x }" ),
.B left / right
delimiters as plain grouping,
.B sub
folded into one identifier,
a diacritical mark folded into one identifier, and
.B sub
immediately followed by
.B sup
on the same name.
.PP
Also covers five deliberate refusals with the specific error each
should give:
.BR sum / from / to
(no computational meaning even in real
.IR eqn ),
.B matrix
(no hoc array type),
a quoted string (a troff label, not a value),
.B over
with nothing before it, and a
.B sub
whose subscript is not a simple name.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/eqn2hoctest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR eqn2hoc (2),
.IR hoc (2),
.IR hoctest (1)
.SH BUGS
Checks the constructs
.IR eqn2hoc (2)
supports and the constructs it refuses, each with a real hand-computed
expected value or a real matching error message \(em it does not fuzz
the parser, and does not exercise every keyword
.IR eqn2hoc (2)
itself lists as unsupported (only a representative one from each of
the reasons given there: no computational meaning, no array type, a
troff-only construct, and a bare operator).
